{
  "id": "eks-multi-cluster-architecture",
  "name": "EKS multi-cluster architecture",
  "category": "Containers & Kubernetes",
  "difficulty": "Advanced",
  "description": "Two independent EKS clusters, Alpha and Beta, in us-east-1. Each has its own AWS-managed control plane, Kubernetes API endpoint, etcd state, controllers and scheduler, plus a separate customer VPC with two EC2 workers and three illustrated pods in private subnets. The administrator uses separate cluster contexts. Management links are editable illustrations, not executable API calls. Start Send Request at Alpha Application Service, then select Beta Application Service as the start resource to test it independently. There is no peering, shared Service discovery, traffic replication or automatic cross-cluster failover. API authorization, scheduler/reconciliation, node-to-pod failure propagation, image pulling and etcd consensus are outside current coverage. Communication arrows show the API server as the hub. The endpoint is its access address, not a worker-management component. Controllers and scheduler watch/update API objects; etcd persists state. Kubelets watch assigned pods and report status via the private endpoint; API server-to-kubelet links illustrate logs, attach and port-forward, not pushing pod scheduling commands.",
  "learningOutcome": "Distinguish multiple Availability Zones in one cluster from multiple independent clusters. Observe independent readiness and desired replica configuration; making Alpha pods NotReady does not change Beta. Separate VPCs use non-overlapping 10.70.0.0/16 and 10.71.0.0/16 CIDRs. In production, inter-cluster connectivity, identity, ingress routing, failover and data replication require explicit design. Source: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/eks-architecture.html",
  "scenario": {
